00:14:07.630 --> 00:14:09.630
That was our Lennon-McCartney moment, Angela.

349
00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:10.690
Oh, wow.

350
00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:15.570
So, Stephen, I know that you went in to interview for a job to be Ricky's assistant at a radio

351
00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:16.010
station.

352
00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:17.770
You walk in the door.

353
00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:19.790
You meet this man.

354
00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:21.210
Did you feel it?

355
00:14:21.610 --> 00:14:22.810
Was there a spark?

356
00:14:23.030 --> 00:14:25.110
Was there a shift in the universe?

357
00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:32.950
Well, it was interesting because, as you say, I did indeed go into a building to meet

358
00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:37.190
him, but he very quickly said, let's go to the pub for a pint of beer.

359
00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:38.110
Oh, my God.

360
00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:38.930
This is my guy.

361
00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:41.010
And there aren't many bosses that do that.

362
00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:42.010
He was hiring me for the job.

363
00:14:42.030 --> 00:14:44.070
So, I was immediately charmed by that.

364
00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:46.530
I thought that sort of seemed like the right approach.

365
00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:50.110
And we went down the road and had a pint and we got chatting.

366
00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:54.530
And we seemed to have a lot of sort of common loves of things like music and comedy and stuff.

367
00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:56.510
So, we hit it off in that regard.

368
00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:59.390
But then he said something to me, which I think was revealing.

369
00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:03.610
He said, listen, this was a radio station where we were both going to work.

370
00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:05.350
And he said, listen, I don't know anything about radio.

371
00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:07.970
I've sweet-talked my way into this job.

372
00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:09.530
I don't know what I'm doing.

373
00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:12.290
And you seem to have had a little radio experience.

374
00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:16.030
If you do all the work, I'll make sure you have fun.

375
00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:21.390
And I, in need of a job, thought, that sounds like a fair deal.

376
00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:26.050
And so, I joined the station with Ricky as essentially my boss.

377
00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:28.790
And true to his word, he did not know what he was doing.

378
00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:34.290
But we managed to find a sort of working relationship.

379
00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:38.390
And after a little while, we were behind the scenes guys.

380
00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:43.010
But after a little while, they realized that our better asset was being on the radio.

381
00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:47.130
And once we went on the radio and we started talking and being kind of presenters,

382
00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:50.090
kind of like you guys, that was when I think we were all starting to make more sense.

383
00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:53.310
And I think that was where our, if you like, our sense of humor

384
00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:55.710
and our relationship with each other and our rapport,

385
00:15:56.010 --> 00:15:59.610
I think that's where it really began to develop, sort of on the radio.

386
00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:03.490
And then from then, it was sort of, you know, it was plain sailing, really.

387
00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:04.730
You know, and we juSt...

388
00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:06.310
And so much of what we, our experience,

389
00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:09.230
so much of sort of getting to know someone that you end up working with in that way

390
00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:11.110
is, I think, just as I'm sure you guys discovered,

391
00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:12.890
it's just finding all the things you have in common.

392
00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:15.210
And I suppose the things you don't like, you know,

393
00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:17.730
and sort of deciding that these are the movies we like,

394
00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:19.230
these are the comedy we like, this is what we don't.

395
00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:23.110
And I think that so much of the initial sort of partnering was that,

396
00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:24.950
was finding that stuff, that common ground.

397
00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:27.130
Oh man, that is such a great story.

398
00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:30.910
And also, I feel like something Angela and I have discovered in working together

399
00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:33.030
is like our strengths and weaknesses

400
00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:37.250
and how they, and our differences in our, in our work.

401
00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:40.430
We both have strong work ethics, but we work differently.

402
00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:46.390
What do you think were some of those things that made you and Ricky a good duo?

403
00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:50.510
Well, I think I was a little younger than Ricky.

404
00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:54.370
And so I had a sort of youthful ambition

405
00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:57.390
and I kind of get up and go sort of dynamism.

406
00:16:57.450 --> 00:16:59.270
And at the time we were working at this radio show

407
00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:02.510
and then I would go off and do stand-up comedy in the evenings and things,

408
00:17:02.630 --> 00:17:05.710
whereas Ricky would sort of be home eating cheese with his girlfriend

409
00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:07.590
by sort of, you know, 6pm.

410
00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:09.370
And I think, you know, he was older

411
00:17:09.370 --> 00:17:11.730
and he'd sort of settled into a certain groove.

412
00:17:12.090 --> 00:17:13.970
And I think I had this kind of eagerness

413
00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:15.610
to sort of make my stamp on the world.

414
00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.110
And I think, but at the same time,

415
00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:20.070
I think what Ricky had was just a sort of untapped

416
00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:22.410
kind of explosive brilliance

417
00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:26.030
that was just sort of waiting to be sort of mined, you know?

418
00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:28.290
And I think I had this slightly more kind of

419
00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:31.910
structured, slightly more rigid approach to things.

420
00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:34.570
And I think it was his sort of his wild spontaneity

421
00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:39.030
and my kind of slightly more, you know, by the book approach.

422
00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:40.770
I think kind of bringing those two things together

423
00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:42.750
sort of worked well, you know?

424
00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:44.830
And I was able to kind of chaperone him

425
00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:47.630
through some of the more boring bits of writing, if you like,

426
00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:49.590
the working of the plots and all those sort of things.

427
00:17:49.970 --> 00:17:51.990
But at the same time, he had a kind of,

428
00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:54.290
he was just sort of smart.

429
00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:56.430
He was just wise about people and about,

430
00:17:56.710 --> 00:17:58.190
you know, he just, he was just older.

431
00:17:58.330 --> 00:17:59.910
And so there was a big brother quality to him

432
00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:01.530
that he could bring to it as well.

433
00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:04.370
So it was just a really interesting dynamic.

434
00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:05.750
We sort of supported each other

435
00:18:05.750 --> 00:18:07.030
and we kind of, I think,

436
00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:09.170
sparked each other's creativity in different ways.

437
00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:11.550
It was just a very exhilarating

438
00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.270
sort of time in both our lives, I think.

439
00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:16.110
Steven, you're my Jenna.

440
00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:17.310
You're my Jenna.

441
00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:17.810
Well, there you go.

442
00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:19.050
That's what I was going to say.

443
00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:20.590
I'm you, Steven.

444
00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:24.750
I'm the structure and the tasks

445
00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:27.850
and Angela's the ba-boom-boom.

446
00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:29.370
I'm like, let's go grab a pint.

447
00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:31.170
Let's bounce some ideas off.

448
00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:32.870
And I'm like, I'll get a pint with you,

449
00:18:32.950 --> 00:18:33.970
but I'm bringing a notebook.

450
00:18:34.450 --> 00:18:34.610
Yeah.

451
00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:36.670
Right, exactly, yeah.

452
00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:40.370
Well, I remember meeting you for the first time

453
00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:44.990
when you came to the set of The Office early, early.

454
00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:46.210
Jenna, where was that?

455
00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:47.370
Where did you guys meet?

456
00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:49.230
Was it the original Culver City set?

457
00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:50.570
Wasn't it?

458
00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:51.550
Steven, do you remember?

459
00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:54.950
I definitely think it was a season one.

460
00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:58.090
Yeah, and we weren't on those sound stages yet, right?

461
00:18:58.090 --> 00:18:59.110
We were in an actual office building.

462
00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:00.590
I think it was a real, that's right.

463
00:19:00.590 --> 00:19:02.970
It was a real office above a real factory.

464
00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:03.510
Is that right?

465
00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:04.470
Or a real warehouse?

466
00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:05.850
Above a real warehouse.

467
00:19:06.170 --> 00:19:06.330
Yeah.

468
00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:07.710
And didn't you guys, Steven,

469
00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:09.530
when you filmed the BBC version,

